 "meta": {
  "description": "Installed generation capacity by province, MW. Thermal from the station-level fleet register (BASE scenario); wind and utility solar from the regional renewable capacity compilation; rooftop from the distributed PV estimate.",
  "note": "CAPACITY, not generation. A province with 30 GW of coal produces far more energy than one with 3 GW of solar at the same nameplate, because the capacity factors differ by a factor of four or five. Read the energy shares alongside, never the megawatts alone.",
  "licence": "CC BY-NC-ND 4.0",
  "attribution": "GridTwin ZA - gridtwinza.org",
  "generation_note": "Generation is MODELLED, not measured. Capacity factors for wind and solar come from the regional hourly profiles, so a Hydra Central wind MW is credited at 42.5% and a Kwazulu Natal one at 21.6%. Thermal factors are derived from the model's own base-case dispatch rather than assumed: coal at 0.434 reproduces the 160.7 TWh the engine actually produces. An assumed 60% put national generation 27% above national demand, which is how you know it was wrong. National total reconciles to 98% of demand; the gap is imports and pumped storage, which are not allocated by province.",
  "demand_note": "Provincial demand is national demand apportioned by the load shares the regional model uses. It is an allocation, not a measurement."
 },
